 <LegalLayout title="Privacy Policy">
-    <h2>1. An overview of data protection</h2>
-    <p><strong>General information</strong></p>
-    <p>The following information will provide you with an easy to navigate overview of what will happen with your personal data when you visit our website. The term “personal data” comprises all data that can be used to personally identify you. For detailed information about the subject matter of data protection, please consult our Data Protection Declaration, which we have included beneath this copy.</p>
-    
-    <h2>2. General information and mandatory information</h2>
-    <p><strong>Data protection</strong></p>
-    <p>The operators of this website and its pages take the protection of your personal data very seriously. Hence, we handle your personal data as confidential information and in compliance with the statutory data protection regulations and this Data Protection Declaration.</p>
-    <p>Whenever you use this website, a variety of personal information will be collected. Personal data comprises data that can be used to personally identify you. This Data Protection Declaration explains which data we collect as well as the purposes we use this data for. It also explains how, and for which purpose the information is collected.</p>
-    
-    <h2>3. Recording of data on our website</h2>
-    <p><strong>Cookies</strong></p>
-    <p>In some instances, our website and its pages use so-called cookies. Cookies do not cause any damage to your computer and do not contain viruses. The purpose of cookies is to make our website more user friendly, effective and more secure. Cookies are small text files that are placed on your computer and stored by your browser.</p>
+    <h2>1. Overview</h2>
+    <p>This page explains what personal data The DevTools Times processes when you use this website, why, and what choices you have. "Personal data" means any information that can identify you. We follow three principles: we collect only what a feature needs, anything optional is off until you turn it on, and we tell you plainly what happens. The organisation responsible for this website is listed in the <a href={`${baseUrl}legal/imprint`}>Imprint</a>.</p>
+
+    <h2>2. What we process and why</h2>
+
+    <p><strong>Reading the site</strong></p>
+    <p>You can read every article without an account and without accepting any optional processing. Like any web server, ours receives your IP address and browser details in order to deliver pages. Our fonts and most images are hosted on our own servers. A few article images load from the image host imgur.com. When you open one of those articles, your browser connects to imgur, which receives your IP address but not the full address of the page you are reading.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Reading list</strong></p>
+    <p>If you save an article to your reading list, we set one session cookie. It lets the server remember which articles you saved. This cookie is strictly necessary for the reading list, and we only set it once you use that feature. It contains only a random identifier.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Your privacy choices</strong></p>
+    <p>We save your answer to the privacy prompt in your browser's local storage (key <code>dt-privacy-consent</code>) so we don't ask again on every page. It never leaves your device.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Accounts (sign up and sign in)</strong></p>
+    <p>To create an account we ask only for a username and a password. We don't ask for your name, email address or any other details, and you can use a nickname. Both are sent over an encrypted (HTTPS) connection to our account service, which runs on Google Cloud. They're used only to create your account and sign you in.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Optional analytics (only with your consent)</strong></p>
+    <p>If you choose <em>Accept all</em> or allow analytics in your privacy choices, we load an analytics script from our analytics service on Google Cloud. When you click a link or button, it records:</p>
+    <ul>
+        <li>the type of element (link or button),</li>
+        <li>the element's visible text, and</li>
+        <li>the time of the click.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>It doesn't record what you type into forms, clicks inside comment sections, or clicks on the sign-up, sign-in or privacy controls. If you don't consent, the script never loads.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Advertising</strong></p>
+    <p>The page layout includes space for ads, but no advertising or ad-tracking scripts are loaded. We also tell your browser to block its built-in ad-targeting and interest-tracking features (such as the Topics API) on this site.</p>
+
+    <h2>3. Browser privacy signals</h2>
+    <p>If your browser sends a <a href="https://globalprivacycontrol.org/">Global Privacy Control</a> signal, we treat it as a refusal of optional analytics and don't show the privacy prompt. You can still turn analytics on yourself if you want to.</p>
+
+    <h2>4. Changing your mind</h2>
+    <p>You can change or withdraw your consent at any time with the <a href="#privacy-preferences" data-privacy-preferences data-skip-tracking="true">Manage Privacy Preferences</a> link at the bottom of every page. If you withdraw consent, analytics stops straight away. You can clear the reading-list cookie and your stored choice at any time by deleting this site's data in your browser settings.</p>
+
+    <h2>5. Data shared with others</h2>
+    <p>We don't sell personal data or share it for advertising. The data described above is processed only by the providers we use to run this site (hosting, our account service and, if you consent, our analytics service). We also send your browser instructions to share only our domain name, never the full address of the page you were reading, when you follow a link to another website.</p>
+
+    <h2>6. Your rights</h2>
+    <p>Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct or delete your personal data, to restrict or object to how it's processed, to get a copy of it (data portability), and to withdraw consent. You can also complain to a data protection authority. To exercise these rights, contact us using the details in the <a href={`${baseUrl}legal/imprint`}>Imprint</a>.</p>
 </LegalLayout>
